United States: Repeating Is Mother of Wisdom
He even became comical, the assiduous effort with which Barack Obama, president of a sage, reproduced his predecessor’s strategy on the Afghan issue, which he himself considered quite simple.
When it ended up letting his doctrine so ardently empty, it turned out that he had copied it with carbon-paper since Bush’s Iraqi script three years ago.
Unpleasant to use in the text even several times the expression 'like in Iraq', he has not hesitated, moreover, to use the title of Bush's 2007 Speech as the title of his own speech: 'A New Road Forward.
At the same time, he personally criticizes Bush’s idea of sending new reinforcements to an Iraq consummated by civil-warency as “irresponsible and a catastrophic decision in its consequences.”
The next few months, however, had shown that Bush’s strategy, which emanated from the recommendations of campaign commanders, was the only one possible and likely to succeed to such an extent that newspapers at the moment did not talk about Iraq at all.
This did not improve Bush’s reputation, that said, objectively speaking, he gave his successor a much more favourable final situation than we could hope.
Barack Obama Hopes the Miracle Will Recreate in Afghanistan
Campaign commanders once again called for an increase in the military contingent, including the commander of the Afghan operation, General Stanley McCristal, who even severely criticized White House reticence despite habits of sub-ordination.
It requested 4 40,000 troops in order to be able to make the situation more favourable.
The president hesitated for three months, but in the absence of a better option, he at least defrauded the general of 10,000 soldiers.
It succeeded in extricating an additional EUR 5 000 promise from Europe, even though it originally stood at 10 000.
What brings the most problems in all of Obama’s strategy seems to be its intention to withdraw the armies of Afghanistan within 18 months and complete the entire process within a three-year period.
Many doubt that an increase by half will yield the same fruits as the advance in Iraq.
Despite fratricidal wars in recent years, Iraqi society is relatively well-organised and its rulers are honouring the agreements.
The situation in Afghanistan stands out by the exact opposite - no one can be relied upon and no treaty is respected.
In these circumstances, it is difficult to prepare Afghan security forces accustomed to changing leaders on a day-to-day basis depending on what suits them.
Political institutions, although officially founded, will remain nothing but dull behind which patriarchal ethnic relations will proliferate.
It is singularly complex to balance the interests of the various ethnic groups in view of the lack of clarity as regards the reports, which are further complicated by the influences of Pakistanis and Iran.
Even the foundation of a "prompt state to act", which is the basic purpose of the operation, does not seem to be very real.
The time horizon of Obama’s strategy perpetuates its voluntarism.
Over the past eight years, the situation in Afghanistan has only worsened and is now on the verge of chaos.
Only a purely romantic, out-of-rescene heart can be persuaded that the small soldiers sent into the tumult of the tumult with a view to their "prompt return to the house" will bring a real sprawphrety.
From a political point of view, defining a date for the withdrawal of troops is purely irresponsible, since the Taliban will regard it as an indirect admission of American defeat.
The most recent NATO summit had shown that the willingness of Europeans to participate in military operations in Afghanistan was oriented towards the negative.
European policies do not know or want to explain to their constituents how security in Germany or Italy is linked to a war in the Hindu Kush mountains.
Another factor capable of radically altering the situation is the evolution of Iranian-related events.
If the situation continues to decelebrate and the increased sanctions do not have an impact on Iranians, the United States will be faced with the very difficult issue of resolving the problem through the use of military force.
It will be necessary to respond, for example, when, according to Obama, America comes to the victorious withdrawal of troops from Afghanistan.
However, every such solution will lead to a dramatic reversal of the Afghan situation.
Obama’s only snack is the fact that there are three years left until the presidential election, and therefore there is still enough time for him to work on a new strategy, in case it turns out to be a total fiasco.
