Hyundai listened to the trade trade unions and canceled every overtime
The Hyundai Plant Management in Nosovice agreed with the trade unions to rescind every overtime.
It was precisely as a result of frequent over-hours that the trade unions declared an emergency strike state on Monday.
Spokeswoman Petr Vanak of the Hyundai plant confirmed this.
However, the management wants to agree again with the trade unions on a Saturday worked again this year.
We can't give up our production plan, it's essential, that's why voluntary overtime hours were offered on Saturday, 19 December knowing that there would be two teams, said Vanik.
It stated that the company offered a bonus of Czech kroons 400 to employees who would be part of Saturday's team.
It will also be awarded postéri for the day worked on 28 November.
According to Vanak, the company has decided to change the motivating way of motivating its employees in exchange for overtime.
Either they receive the supplement for the extra hours, or they can take an alternative holiday on 28 December, thereby prolonging their end-of-year festivities indicated Vanik.
In addition, all employees present on Saturdays will receive compensation for transport costs and for their lunch.
Taking into account the fact that overtime was the most urgent and hottest point in the comments and demands of trade unions such as employees who spontaneously ceased production last week, the management made an immediate decision to rescind every overtime for the month of December.
Until last week, employees at the automobile factory were forced to work almost two extra hours a day in more than eight hours.
The trade unions intend to rescind the state of the strike after signing the agreement.
The next negotiation between the management of the car factory and the trade unions is to take place on Friday afternoon.
Trade union leader Petr Kuchaai said on Wednesday that they were ready to stop the strike in the event of an agreement on their claims and the signing of a joint document.
The situation in the factory culminated last Wednesday when nearly 400 employees spontaneously collided due to unceasing overflight hours.
They were also protesting against poor wage and so-called hazing conditions.
The management defended itself by the fact that employees had to work additionally to meet the requirements for the production of cars.
When the strike was announced on Monday, the trade unions also called for no sanctions to be sanctioned by employees who stopped production.
Trade unions are calling on the society to minimize the time spent in overtime.
In addition, this year they want a recompense of 5,000 kroons (about 209 dollars) for them.
The Hyundai Car Factory is currently employing 2 000 people.
It started mass production last year in November.
It manufactured about 80,000 vehicles until September this year, with a production capacity of 20,000 cars per year.
Czech Discovery: A Substance Relating to "Wilting" Virgury Virus
A team of Czech and German scientists is testing new combinations that can slow down the proliferation of the HIV virus in the body.
The main advantage of the improved substance is that it also acts on viruses that have already developed immunity to medicines.
In some cases, this reality takes precedence over the fact that the combination does not act as intensely on the common variants of the virus as well as certain medicines already available.
It is not possible to cure AIDS at the present time.
However, the lives of the patients can be radically prolonged via a mixture of medicines that prevent the proliferation of the HIV virus in the body.
Their consumption, however, leads to a number of side-effects.
In addition, the repression of this multiplication has the effect of developing so-called resistant viruses on which the medicaments no longer have an effect.
The work of experts from three institutions, the Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic (CHT in Prague) and the University of Heidelberg paved the way for dealing with virus resistance.
They have shown that substances called metalocarboranes act on the protein responsible for multiplying the HIV virus.
Metalocarborane is an assembly of bo Boron, Hydrogen, Carbon and Cobalt.
This combination blocks the development of the virus in a different way than all the drugs used so far and that is why they could overcome the problem of resistance.
The work of the researchers led them to undispose of "improved" new substances, based on their knowledge of the molecular mechanics of their virgins on virus proteins.
Metallocarborans have a single three-dimensional structure: two multi-walled cages are formed by boron atom atoms of bo bo Boron, Hydrogen and Carbon and connected by a Metal atom, in this case cobalt.
A treacherous and resistant virus.
HIV Protease is an HIV virus protein which is essential to the life cycle of the virus.
Without the division of the HIV virus caused by the HIV Protebate there would be no mature infectious particles of the virus.
Stopping the HIV Protebational Protease will also stop the spread of the virus in the patient's body, the researchers explain in a press release published by the Institute of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry of the Czech Academy of Sciences of the Czech Republic.
In their work published in the scientific journal Journal of Medicinal Chemistry, the scientists describe a series of combinations in which two pairs of cages are linked by a short organic link, which is then systematically modified.
A little lower but more reliable.
The effectiveness of this series of substances on HIV proteases has been tested in test specimens as well as its resistant variants obtained from HIV-contaminated patients.
On the current version of the enzyme, the effect of metalocarboranes is not as strong as on clinical drugs, but it does not weaken on resistant versions against which common drugs are often ineffective.
Metallo-carboranes, with their unique mechanism of action and other properties such as their biological and chemical industral stability, their low toxicity and the possibility of other chemical modifications, are interesting combinations to be examined further towards the development of effective anti-HIV drugs, says Pavlena zezova, director of the Laboratory of Structural Bisology of Organic Chemistry and Biochemistry and the Institute of Genetics.
