Depending on the new flight hours, there will be fewer trains for identical fares.
The Czech Railways will eliminate or limit from 13 December certain express or regional less loaded trains and will instead strengthen some others.
Total transport coverage will fall on the day of entry into force of new timetables by two per cent compared to today.
Most fares will not be changed, including basic and reduced fares.
It is for example the inhabitants of the Ostrava region who will have more connections to the capital, the Chemins de Iron will add a pendolino train.
On the other hand, an express will stop serving Bratislava.
There will also be fewer high-speed trains on the Prague-Psek-Peské Budojovice route, the Prague-Letohrad direct line will be drastically reduced.
At the regional level, the largest reduction in transport concerns the Hradec Kralové region by a total of eight per cent, on the other hand, Prague and its surroundings can rejoice at an increase.
Two overnight trains are also cancelled and the circulation of a few express trains on the Prague-Pusek-Peské Budojovice route will be reduced to just a few days a few days in the week.
Among the greatest transformations, one can see the introduction of a new Milovice direct line in Prague: for the time being, the inhabitants of Milovice will have to change to Lyso nad Labem.
This is the fifteenth line of the Esko subterranean subterranean system, which is possible thanks to the completion of electrification of the line between Lyosa nad Labem and Milovice.
Disparture lines to the capital will have their final destination at the main railway station in Prague (Praha Hlavna ndra).
It will introduce more SC Pendolino lines between Prague and Ostrava and trains bound for zumperk/Jesen-k will depart at two-hour intervals.
Travellers will no longer be able to take the Pendolino to Bratislava from 13 December.
The bill this year for the regional railways is an additional 200 million kroons.
Express trains are ordered and paid for by the Ministry of Transport and will pay to the Chemins de fer over a total of four billion kroons next year to settle the visible losses, the same amount as this year. The regional trains are being ordered by the regions that will pay a total of eight billion for them next year, knowing that the state will contribute almost 3 billion.
This year regional trains are EUR 200 million more expensive.
Chemlands have newly signed a ten-year contract, so far the duration has still been one-and-a-half.
The railways appreciate the system, which also explains why they did not resort to an increase in the prices of the vast majority of transport tickets.
The changes will only affect one-day 'D Net tickets' and discounted internet tickets.
For passengers without a discount card, the price will be increased from 450 to WrK.
The price of eLi-ka will recently depend on the route travelled, whereas until then it was valid between cities of a region of your choice and was at a one-daily fare of kroons 160.
