Labour defends an increase in the budget tax.
The ministers defended the increase of the tax and the spending cuts announced in the pre-Budget report, despite criticism from opposition, companies and trade unions.
The Tories claimed that Alistair Darling "failed" an opportunity to show that it was serious about reducing the deficit by postponing its decisions until after the elections.
The Cancellor also wrinkled thrilled by attacking low-wage and middle-worries.
Its main proposals include an increase of 0.5% in Social Insurance and a 1 per cent ceiling on public wages starting in 2011.
The Angrill of Social Insurance
The trade unions protested that low-worsets were penalized by a recession for which they were not responsible, and cautioned that "problems" announced.
Declining Social Insurveele – which will achieve about GBP 3 billion a year – has angered the business world, because, in his view, it is an employment tax when the objective should be a recovery of the economy.
The increase, reserved for those earning more than GBP 20,000 a year, will affect around 10 million workers.
PRINCIPLED UNIPOSES OF THE PRE-BUDGET REPORT
Social insurance will increase further by 0.5% from April 2011
The economy will fall even more than 4.75% expected this year
New 50 per 50 per cent tax on banker bonuses
Rabing corporate income tax for small companies has been phased out
Duties of taxes on electric cars and wind turbines
-2.5% increase in pensions
According to treasury estimates, a person earning GBP 30,000 will lose GBP 9 90 per year and a GBP 40,000 will lose GBP 190, while one person earning GBP 10,000 per year will earn an additional GBP 110.
The Ministers stated that their target of halving the deficit before 2013 led to "difficult decisions" but stressed that 60% of the additional tax burden would fall on the top 5% of employees.
Stephen Timms, Secretary of the Treasurer, refused an increase in the tax and pointed out that a reduction in expenditure was a "drop of water into the ocean" compared to what was needed.
'These numbers are important, and they will allow us to halve the deficit, which will be absolutely essential over the next four years,' he told the BBC.
Mr. Timms said that he was confident and that the growth of the economy would recover at the end of the year.
However, Labour’s forecast of economic growth – which will influence the amount it will have to borrow and the speed with which the deficit will be closed – were in doubt after the Chancellor was forced to revise its first figures.
In his speech, he stated that the economy would probably fall by 4.75% this year – far worse than the 3.5% decline predicted in April – while the loan would be 3 billion GDP higher than the previous estimate.
"Fainted opportunity"
Preservatives who will launch on Thursday a new publicity campaign alerting the “debt crisis” of Labour, have claimed that the project to borrow GBP 789 billion over the next six years is not viable.
Ministers failed to put forward a credible plan on redeeming, they added, evading difficult choices until after the elections - to be held in June 2010.
"They didn't present anything meaningful about what they were going to do," said Philip Hammond, Chief Secretary of the Shadow of the Shadow Cabinet. "They had an opportunity to do it and they missed it."
"Strong announcements will be made after the elections, regardless of the winner."
Roger Bootle, Deloitte
Labour insists that schools, hospitals and the police will be protected from future reductions, in contrast to Tory proposals, which would lead to a faster and greater reduction in the deficit.
But the Lib Dems claimed that the money raised from rising taxes and lower spending would be used to support next year’s spending and not to reduce the deficit, arguing that the plans were “built on the wind.”
Nick Robinson, BBC Political Editor, has argued that the timing of advertisements – such as the increase in inflation for certain disability benefits next April, mentioned above – would leave the Chancellor in the face of propagatility charges, with the money simply recovered the following year.
However, Labour insists that additional assistance is being provided when people are most in need and that the situation will be reviewed in September 2010.
False budget
Although ministers did not publish the details of spending beyond 2011, economists argued that it was clear that restrictions on public spending would only begin to be felt in the medium term.
The well-respected Institute for  Fiscal Studies has stated that the Chancellor’s figures imply, in the future, substantial reductions in many areas, potentially involving transport, higher education, science and defence.
"Pain is in fact only postponed," said its Director, Robert Chote.
'The most important advertisements will be made after the elections, regardless of the victor", added Roger Bootle, Deloitte Economic Adviser for Accountants.
'This was a falsifying pre-budget report.
