The FBI is investigating the arrest in Pakistan of five U.S. reporters suspected of extremist ties.
The men were arrested during a raid on a house in Sarghoda in the eastern province of Punjab, the U.S. Embassy in Pakistan told the BBC.
The FBI stated that it was investigating whether it was the same men who had been declared missing from their home in the U.S. state of Virginia last month.
U.S. Foreign Ministry has said that it is also looking for information about these people.
Three of them are described as being of Pakistani ancestry, one of Egyptian inheritances and the other of Yemeni origin.
If they are American citizens, of course, we'd like to know what the charges against them are and under what conditions they're being detained," said Spokesman Ian Kelly.
Katherine Schweit, an FBI Spokeswoman, stated that the agency was aware of the arrests and was in contact with the families of the missing students.
'We are working together with the Pakistani authorities to determine their identity and the nature of their activities there, and whether it is really the students who are missing,' she said.
The Pakistani Embassy in D.C. claimed that the men had been arrested in a house belonging to the unscrupulous uncle of one of them.
It stated that the house was already being monitored by the local police and that no charges had yet been brought against the arrested men.
Secretary of State Hillary Clinton denied any comment on the arrests, the Reuters news agency reported, but claimed that the United States had to "more work with Afghanistan and Pakistan in an attempt to uproot the terrorist infrastructure that continues to recruit and train people".
The five students had been dispossessed by their families in northern Virginia at the end of November.
Amnesty Congratifies Iranian 'abuses'
Over the past 20 years, human human rights have been as poorly respected as ever, according to Amnesty International's report.
the report presents 'rehesets of violations' by the regime, both before and after the campaign for the presidential elections in June.
A man quoted in the report stated that he had been beaten and burned with cigarettes; another stated that he had been threatened with staunches.
Iran has denied previous critics of its non-observance of human rights.
The authorities have stated that such critics are politically motivated.
Thousands of people had been arrested and dozens had been killed in Iran, after the contested election of President Mahmoud Ahmadinejad led to the largest public demonstrations since the 1979 Islamic revolution.
Dozens had been sentenced to prison terms, and prosecutors claimed that at least five people had been sentenced to death.
Jon Leyne, a BBC correspondent in Tehran, now in London, claims that at the beginning of the protests, Ayatollah Ayatollah Ali Khamenei acknowledged some of the allegations of abuse, ordering the closure of the Kahrizak detention centre.
But since then, the authorities did not tolerate any criticism, says our correspondent.
False Confession
Amnesty International recounted the case of a 26-year-old computer science student, Ebrahim Mehtari, who claimed to have been accused of "Working with Facebook networks" and of protesting against the election results.
'They often hit me in the face,' he was quoted.
"I've been burned with cigarettes, under my eyes, on my neck, in my head... They threatened to execute and humiliated me."
After five days, he signed a falsic confession and was released and left on the street, still in blood and half-conscious, said Amnesty.
In August, the losing candidate in the elections, Mehdi Karroubi, claimed that protesters detained after the election had been tortured to death in prison and that others had been raped.
The Iranian authorities denied the charges of rape, but admitted that abuses had taken place.
International staff also cited the case of a former indetainee who said that he had been locked up in a container with 75 others for more than eight weeks in a Kahrizak detention centre for more than eight weeks.
AI accepted that the Iranian Parliament and the judiciary should set up a committee to investigate post-election disturbances and the government's response, but it stated that the mandate and powers of these bodies were not clear and that the results of the parliamentary committee had not been made public.
The group stated that at least 90 people had been arrested over the past three weeks in an attempt to prevent further demonstrations.
