Can Islam be French?
Pluralism and Pragmatism in a secular state.
Roger Bootle, Deloitte
Some readers will be lurned by this new book, admirers of the author's latest book, "While the French don't like headscarves" (2006), an elegant and well-documented study on a problem that has divided and preoccupying the country for about 15 years and whose effects are still being felt today.
Mr. Bowen's latest book has a much broader and more ambitious framework.
Good anthropologist, he doesn't only want to know what politicians and the media are saying about Islam in France, but what's currently going on in the country.
He has spent many months in mosques, schools and institutes that are now supplying in France what Bozen calls “Muslim spaces” for the 5m-6m of Muslims.
He knows very well how to listen and repropose debates between teachers and students on the issues that concern them the most.
Must a Muslim get married in a mosque or mayairie (or both)?
Should the development and rights of homosexuals be taught to young Muslims?
Could a Muslim woman marvelle a non-Muslim?
Can a Muslim use an interest-based banking system to obtain a loan?
It is these practical problems, he says, which are the day-to-day lord, rather than political drammies of concern in the media.
The Author identifies a new generation of Imams, Teachers and Incentacists, all unknown to the general public, except perhaps Tariq Ramadan, an Arab Muslim born in Switzerland.
This new generation is trying to open the debate on how to be both a good Muslim and a good citizen in a modern secular society.
They don't push the debate all the way.
Preservatives do not believe in the idea of French or European Islam.
Intellectuals and activists who have been interviewed by Bowen are generally in agreement with their lawyers of a Sunni Sunni Purist Islam associated with Saudi Arabia, which today make up a small proportion of Muslims in Europe.
Bowen believes that the values of Muslims and the secularism of the French could be compatible.
But a compromise can only be achieved with the efforts of each side.
He wonders how much French politicians (and the virligant intellectual guardian of secularism) are truly committed to pluralism.
