Learned the lesson that the authorities of the Shanghai Government, alarmed by the insufficient birth rate of their city and the aging of the population, radically changed direction this summer and started to encourage young couples to have more than one child; their reaction was instantaneous and firm: never.
We've already donated all our time and energy to a single child.
"We don't have any seconds left," said Wang, 31, Human Resources Director and an eight-month-a-half-year-old son.
More than 30 years after the introduction of one-child policy in China, creating two unique generations of children notoriously with good cheeks and spoilers, dubbed “little emperors,” a population crisis threatening the country.
According to the United Nations, the average birth rate fell from 1.8 children per couple compared to six when the policy was implemented.
The distribution of the population, whereas it is expected that the number of residents aged 60 and over will blemize, from 16.7 per cent of the population in 2020 to 31.1 per cent in 2050, well above the overall average of about 20 per cent.
Such an imbalance is even worse in rich coastal cities with a highly educated population, such as Shanghai.
Last year, people aged 60 and over accounted for nearly 22 per cent of the resident population registered in Shanghai, compared to less than one child per couple.
Xie Lingli, Director of the Shanghai Municipal Population and Family Planning Commission, stated that fertile couples must have babies in order "to help reduce the proportion of the aging population and to prevent a labour shortage in the future".
Shanghai is on the verge of becoming 'as old - but not as rich, however - as developed countries such as Japan and Sweden", she said.
Gradual attenuation
Renewed in the constitution of the country in 1978, China’s one-child policy is undoubtedly the most controversial obligation introduced by the communist government.
Couples who transgress this policy risk having to pay colossal fines -- up to three times their annual income -- and to be discriminated against in the workplace.
The Chinese authorities had given the police the duty to help the country avoid a trend dangerous for its natural resources, while human rights lawyers had denounced the abuses in the execution of the policy.
In rural areas, some representatives of the authorities had forced pregnant women from a second child to have an abortion.
In addition, a large number of couples have resorted to selective abortions leading to an abnormally tall-fashioned-your-girl ratio.
In recent years, the authorities in charge of the population have gradually softened their position on one-child policy.
In 2004, they gave more exceptions to the rule -- including for urban residents, members of ethnic minorities and the cases in which men and women are already single children -- and in 2007, they softened up many of their tough slogans.
Qiao Xiaochun, a professor at the Institute of People's proficiency research at Peking University, has stated that central government authorities have recently discussed even more radical changes, such as allowing couples to have two children if one of the partners is a single child.
In July, Shanghai became the first Chinese city to launch an Aggregating Campaign to Encourage Births.
In no time, posters inciting families to have only one child had been replaced by copies of retail regulations that had the right to have a second child and how to apply for an authorization.
The city government dispatched family planners and volunteers to meet with couples at their homes and swipe leaflets under their doors.
It also promised to provide psychological and financial advice to those who would like to have more than one child.
According to the family planning manager, the response was disappointing.
Dissunciating response
Although officials in a rural town in the suburbs of Shanghai say they saw an increase in the requests for bribes from couples wanting a second child after the launch of the campaign, the more urban district reported that there was no change.
Huinan commune, for example with a population of 115,000, still receives only four to five requests per month.
Undisappointed Shanghai officials claim that, despite the campaign, the number of births in the city in 2010 is still projected to be only about 165,000 – slightly higher than in 2009, but less in 2009.
Feng Juying, Head of the Family Planning Committee in Caolu Municipality of Shanghai, stated that financial considerations are probably the main reason why many people don't want to have more children.
"They want to provide for their first child," she said.
Yang Jiawei, aged 27 and his wife, Liu Juanjuan, aged 26, stated that they would love to have two children and are legally authorized.
But, like a lot of Chinese, they only have the minimum health insurance and the health co-insurance given by the government.
Without social protection, they say, the decision would be irresponsible.
Westerners misrequently believe that one-child politics is a rights issue, said Yang, a construction engineer whose seven-month-would the couple's first-year-would child.
It's true, we're thrigging the luck of having more than a single child.
But the problem isn't just the law.
It's money.
Other couples cite psychological reasons to explain their hesitation.
Wang, Human Resources Officer, said she only wanted a single child, because she was one herself. "We were the center of the family and fluctuated that everybody takes care of us."
We don't get used to caring for someone else and we really don't want to take care of others."
42-year-year-old Chen zijian and the owner of a translation company puts it bluntly.
Couples working in two, middle-class who lower the birth rate, he says, we must be self-evil capable of succeeding.
Twenty- or 30-year-olds grew up seeing their parents fight in the early days of capitalist China and don't want to have this lifestyle themselves, he says.
Even a single child takes a long time from his parents, he says.
A mother must give up at least two years of her social life.
Then there are space problems -- "you have to change your apartment" -- and planning -- "When your child is only nine months old, you have to write his CV to get a place in the best kindergarten."
Most of his friends are ready for this experience once, says Chen, but not two.
Our generation is the first to benefit from a higher standard of living, he says.
We don't want to make too many sacrifices.
The United Nations group has draft emission reduction plans
The United Nations-sponsored climate-sponsored climate conference -- so far characterised by disorganized assignments and mutual recriminations -- came back to lorry on Friday, due to the publication of a document highlighting the ambitious reductions in greenhouse gases over the next 40 years, with industrialized nations supporting most of the burden in the near future.
The text that could form the basis for a final political agreement to regulate greenhouse gases highlighted the remaining obstacles, as much as it shed light on the path to be taken.
But it has been seen as an important step forward in time-saving negotiations with more than 100 leaders from all over the world arriving next week in Copenhagen.
Prepared by a United Nations ad hoc United Nations working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-level working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-class working-
And it offers a wide range of options for key issues, including the way in which new developed economies and emerging economies could reduce their carbon production and the upper limit of the increase in global temperature that policymakers would be willing to tolerate.
It gives great flexibility to the process, said John Coequyt, Repositor in Washington of the Sierra Club.
Michael Cutajar, who wrote six pages of the document, studied a 180-page negotiating text to focus on what the Executive Secretary-General on United Nations Climate Change, Yvo de Boer, described as "complete picture".
It shows the guidelines of a possible agreement in which industrial nations would collectively reduce their emissions in 2020, from 25 to 45 per cent compared to 1990 levels, while the main developing countries, during the same period, would reduce their emissions by 15 to 30 per cent.
Together, countries would reduce emissions by between 50 and 95 per cent by 2050.
The European Union also re-launched the negotiations on Friday, pledging to donate EUR 3.6 billion a year over the next three years to help the poorest countries adapt to the impact of climate change – to withstand floods and drought or to avoid deforestation.
However, the same verbal altercations took place, which have dominated the negotiations since last week.
Tod Stern, United States representative for climate negotiations, rejected any discussion obliging the reduction of greenhouse gases by the industrialized countries, in comparison with the voluntary efforts made by the major emerging economies if they were not financed by the developed countries.
This change is a signal that the Obama administration is getting tougher on China than the Bush administration was, two years ago.
“The United States is not going to sign an unfasheemed agreement from the main developing countries, says Stern who also regretted that the text did not insist enough to ensure that the reductions could be verified by external observers.
Stern made his comments an hour after China’s foreign-affiliated Vice-Minister He Yafei stated that the US chief negotiator, lacked “greasy sense,” was “extremely irresponsibly” for saying earlier in the week that the United States wouldn’t help China financially to combat global-modern global warming.
With the future economic trajectories of the world’s major powers at stake, flaws have emerged both within the Third World and between the industrial and emerging-economy economies.
The current battle involves rescuing individual-economy-spoilments rather than rescuing the planet, with China and the United States quarrelling their respective obligations, while the poorer nations insist that the two or so more influential countries ignore the scientific imperatives to act more courageously.
Ricardo Ulate, a delegate from Costa Rica, had said that it was not surprising that the major powers were struggling to determine who should bear the costs of reducing greenhouse gases, even though vulnerable countries had become more aggressive in their attempt to hold the major emitters responsible for their actions.
“This is really a game in which new economic hegemony is developed,” said Ulate, who is also Climate Advisor for Mexico and Central America for Conservation International.
Some of these countries more vulnerable to the impact of climate change have indicated that they will continue to put the pressure to obtain a legally binding treaty in Copenhagen, even though most of the main participants say that the negotiations will at least lead to a political agreement.
The Alliance of Small Island Member States of 43 members drafted a 24-page treaty proposal early in Friday morning.
Artur Runge-Metzger, Head of Climate Unity of the European Commission, has said that the actions of small island states have “pressed political pressure on the entire political process”, in part by what they are now unified and are calling on emerging economies such as China and India to act.
The discussions became an urgent matter, while the delegates focused on the fact that they had to resolve most pending problems before the Heads of Government reached an agreement.
High-level officials such as Indian Environment Minister Jairam Ramesh and Chinese Deputy Prime-Canadian Prime-Canadian Airplane and rushed into the Bella Center Beautiful building to attend closed-door meetings and news lectures, in order to make claims that will be investigated over the following week.
The real image of the rally -- where 13,000 people enter and leave the Congressional Palace every day -- guitar activists present night shows mockery of the countries that they believe betray and that proposals are written and distributed on paper rather than by e-mail -- is a real challenge.
Intensity is only increasing: almost all senior ministers are here and no later than on Wednesday, 60 Heads of Government will be in Copenhagen.
“We are entering the yard of the greats,” said Carlos Manuel Rodriguez, Vice-President for Global Politics of Conservation International.
Hunger trucks are coming.
In Great Britain, the first train at 225 km/h will redevelop Monday syndrome
At 05:13 on Monday morning, a whistle will break out at night to signal the departure of a train that will mark a transport revolution in Great Britain.
The country's first high-speed suburbing train will reach 225 km/h, as it dives from Ashford in Kent to London St Pancras, covering 93 km in 38 minutes.
Lord Adonis, Minister of Transport, will be joined by Dame Kelly Holmes aboard Javelin's first sub-carriage outside London.
It hopes that the new service will enable Great United Kingdom to integrate an international network of fast and reliable trains and will link the major conurbations of the Midlands, North and Scotland in London.
Lost small holes will become thriving suburb cities, the duration of business trips will be much less long, and the shift from airplane to train will reduce domestic carbon emissions while reducing traffic congestion on the roads.
In theory, the three main political parties belong to this project.
But the ten billion pounds needed to build a new rail network, the inevitable planning battle, and fears of environmental hazards could curb Britain’s high-velocity lines.
According to The Times, High Speed Two (HS2), a company created in January to produce a feasibility report for the government, will return its document to Lord Adonis on December 30.
This will enable him to obtain a detailed map of the itinerary of the next stage planned for the high-speed network.
The new line between London and the West Midlands will be drawn within five metres of the urban area and sites where environmental impacts may be controversial.
In the countryside, the map will be drawn up to 25 meters from the final itinerary.
It is also expected that HS2 will present three options for a taller high-velocity network, moving to the north.
Lord Adonis will reply in the spring.
Preferred options are highlighted in a Y-configuration for which a high-velocity line goes to the West Midlands.
The line will split in two or near Birmingham, with one branch going west from Pennines to Manchester and Scotland and another going north-east towards Sheffield, Leeds and Newcastle upon Tyne.
A single line will run through Scotland.
'It looks like this will be the best performance option' said a rail professional.
When it is completed, the travel time between London and Edinburgh will be reduced to 2 hours 40 minutes.
The first branch, which will not open until 2025, will allow all passengers to travel between London and Birmingham in 49 minutes, compared to a one-hour journey at present.
The proposals will allow the train to travel at 400 km/h, making the UK network the fastest in the world.
The original layout is designed to allow trains 400 m in length that can carry 1,100 people.
Up to 18 trains per hour can operate on a London-Birmingham route.
A terminal capable of accommodating 20,000 passengers per hour would be required.
Given the space constraints, it is expected that an existing station will be enlarged to receive the high-speed network.
Experts from the rail industry state that only St Pancras International or Euston offer this possibility.
Lord Adonis would announce whether or not he believed that the project should be put in place, but longer public consultations and planning procedures meant that a final decision would not be taken before a general election.
Even if it launches the machine, the network will have to be built part-by-party.
Trains will initially be obliged to drive off high-speed lines on existing rails north of Birmingham, maintaining travel times between London and Scotland greater than three o'clock.
This approach seems to be the best way to ensure a switch from aircraft to trains, constituting a point of reference for the rail revolution.
Taxpayers reimburse GBP 75,000 for Tory anangel
Alan Duncan, the principal member of the Tory Parliament, charged the taxpayer thousands of pounds per year to reimburse the cost of an Orange factory that he built in his constituency.
The party leader who was demoted by David Cameron for complaining that members of parliament were "maintained", increased his loan by GBP 75,000 to build an oak extension at his second home in Rutland.
It had been authorized to charge the taxpayer the additional interest at hundreds of pounds per month.
The Commodity authorities received the complaints at that time and were not interrogated by Sir Thomas Legg, who carried out an inspection of the members' expenses; last night, Mr Duncan stated that the complaints could not be "clarier or simpler".
Mr. Duncan, one of the wealthiest members of parliament, owns a two-storey house in a village in its constituency of Rutland - Melton.
Neighbouring houses were sold for nearly one million pounds.
The ground floor comprises a kitchen, lounge and dining room, but the emolument desk states that Mr. Duncan needed more space.
Last year, he added a veranda to the house, describing it in its plans as an orange grove.
Neighbors describe it as a veranda for pleasure.
Mr. Duncan was not asked to provide an explanation for an increase in borrowing in the course of a recent investigation carried out by the Beneficiaries and Standards Committee concerning its prior borrowing provisions.
The Committee exaggerated it last month of any charge of non-compliance after an inquest into its expenditures.
In 2004, Mr Duncan changed the legal certainty of the loan of GBP 271,406 from his London property to the house in his constituency which he bought in 1991, the year before becoming a member of Parliament.
The documents published this week show that Mr Duncan was claiming about GBP 1,400 per month in borrowing interest until March last year.
In April, its applications increased by more than GBP 1,800 per month.
The interest rate on Mr Duncan's RBS loan was not changed at that time, suggesting that all the increases were intended to finance GBP 75,000.
Variable RBS standard interest rate increased from 7.94 per cent in December 2007 to 4 per cent in March this year without moving since then.
The most recent publication by the Commons authorities shows that Mr Duncan was still requesting GBP 1,250 in May.
Mr Duncan lost his position as Ghost Firm Manager in September, after an undercover journalist supposedly filmed him stating that members of parliament had to survive 'on the budget' after the spending scandal.
"Actually, it's now national, you have to live "on budget" and you're being treated as 'm...e'
He said: "I spent my money on my backyard and I asked for a small part, based on what was correct.
And I couldn't have asked damn more, but I didn't."
The member of parliament, a millionaire since his former post as an oil dealer, began having to explain his expenses, in May, after it became apparent that he had requested thousands of pounds for his garden, before agreeing with the office of the costs that this "could be considered excessive".
An Activist digged a z-shape hole in M Duncan's lawn, protesting after it was revealed that he had requested GBP 4,000 for three years.
Asked about the increase in borrowing last night, Mr Duncan said: 'This was for crucial improvements, approved both by the expense office and by Legg.'
He added: 'There is no problem of evasion or drawing up or using the rules or anything.
It was quite clear, according to the rules, allowed, and so on.
It couldn't be sharper or simpler."
