Separating Abkhazia has elected its president, Georgia denies 'farce'
Altokhaa elected its president on Saturday, just over a year after Moscow’s recognition of the pro-Russian separatist region of Georgia, while Tbilisi denounces a “farce”.
Some 131,000 people were expected to take part in this first election after the Russian-Georgian war of August 2008 for the control of South Ossetia, another Georgian secessionist territory recognized by Russia.
Polling stations closed at 17:00 GMT.
Voter turnout was 5.8 per cent hours prior to the closure, according to officials of the Abkhaz Electoral Commission.
The first provisional results were to be announced during the night from Saturday to Sunday.
The five competing candidates, including the outgoing president, Sergei Bagapch, categorically rejected the idea of reunification with Georgia, which condemned the holding of the vote.
These elections are a joke.
For its part, Georgian President Mikheil Saakashvili, "regret that Abkhazia has fallen under the total occupation of Russia", his spokeswoman, Manana Manjgaladzé said.
In addition to Russia, only Nicaragua and Venezuela have recognized the independence of this territory of 216,000 inhabitants, while the rest of the world sees it as part of Georgia illegally occupied by Russian troops.
After segregating from Georgia during the 1992-1993 conflict, which resulted in several thousand deaths, Abkhazia is trying to demonstrate through this election its readiness to join the international community.
This process is a step in our new life, our new era as an independent state, Svetlana Kvartchia, a 54-year-old historian, who claimed to have voted for Bagapch.
She was among the voters who went to the polling station at a school in Suchhoumi, the capital, in a lovely white building with a few palm trees in front of the entrance.
The issue of Russian domination is at the heart of the presidential elections in this region with sumptuous landscapes on the shore of the Black Sea.
Extensive signs showing Mr. Bagapch alongside the Russian President and Prime Minister, Dmitry Medvedev and Vladimir-Putin, are installed throughout this small territory of 216,000 inhabitants.
In Gali, in the western part of the country, the head of the district, Beslan Archba, warmly welcomed the Russian officials who came to monitor the conduct of the elections.
"We, the people of the Akhaz, are grateful, in a small ceremony, alluding to the support of Moscow, in particular the decision to recognize the independence of Abkhazia.
I hope that your country will soon be recognised by the United Nations, said Alexei Ostrovski, a Member of the Russian Parliament, by raising a glass of vodka.
For its part, the Opposition represented in the elections by former Vice-President Raoul Khadjimba and two businessmen, Ardzinba and Beslan Boutba criticizing the agreements concluded with Moscow last year, such as that ceasing to Russia control over the Abkhaz 10-year-old railway.
The fifth nominee, Vitali Bganba, says it supports neither the current power nor the opposition.
A majority of 50% plus one vote is needed to win the election.
If no candidate reaches this threshold, a second round must be arranged within two weeks.
