A Normale Sup, experts have 15 minutes to persuad the climate
Fifteen minutes to talk about the issues at stake at the Copenhagen Summit: this is the challenge faced by Jean Jouzel, Vice-Chairman of the Intergovernmental Group of Experts on Climate Evolution (GIEC), invited by the Ecole Normale Supérieure de la rue d'Ulm.
Jean Jouzel, also the Nobel Peace Prize laureate, inaugurated the first of the "Ernest" conferences, a day consisting of a sequence of expert interventions of fifteen minutes each, filmed and open to the public, noted an AFP journalist.
We wanted to do something that grabs people and makes them want to find out more, explained on the fringes of the 22-year-old Edouard Hannezo conference, one of the organizers of this day which derives its name from the one donated by the students to fish in the school basin.
Some 20 speakers were selected from among the experts in their disciplines and selected for their charismatic side, he said.
With regard to global warming, "you can talk about hours, but we can also talk about the key objectives in a matter of minutes," Jean Jouzel told AFP, who spoke without notes, but using graphics, in front of about 50 people.
This is part of our role as a scientist, he stressed, hoping that the listeners will remember that 'the Copenhagen conference is based on an extremely serious scientific dossier'.
Videos from these lectures will be made available online on the school website from 15 December.
The next "Ernest" day is scheduled in February, followed by others at a bimonthly pace.
Delay in payment: anger of prison wardens, planned actions
