In the last few days, in the last few days, of the statements that you were going to propose to your political movement the acronym UDF, wrote the association in a letter signed by 19 of the 24 members of the Bureau, including François Bayrou and Marielle de Sarnez du MoDem and Senator Jean Arthuis of the Centrist Alliance.
You have also thought it appropriate to forward on your militant material and on the on-the-shelf page of this movement, the "New Central", of the slogan 'the UDF of today', also note the signatories of the letter.
As you are not aware, you have no right to use this acronym, explains the UDF desk, which requires the CN to withdraw "immediately mentioning the name of the UDF from any medium as well as from (his) website".
It also asks Hervé de Charette "to pass on the property of (the) trademark to its legitimate beneficiaries".
If you didn't do so, the courts would be seized to enforce the law and the legitimate rights of UDF adherents, warn the signatories.
In their letter, they state that the UDF "is a full-fledged political movement since 1978" and point out that Mr Morin and his friends had "pleased to leave it in 2007 "to create a competing movement, as others did in 2002 to create the UMP".
The New Centre, in their view, therefore has no right to use UDF (Union for French Democracy) acronym.
They would also point out that the last UDF congress in 2007 voted to join MoDem for three years and to set up an office responsible for defending the "material and moral interests" of training founded by Valéry Giscard d'Estaing.
As for Hervé de Charette, continues the office, 'when he has registered the trade mark', he was "no longer a member of the UDF for three years", but in the UMP.
It proceeds scandalously and fraudulently from the abscence of the acronym UDF in order to perform it on its behalf.
This indeclination is being transformed into an offence, they denounce, before irooing on the NC's strategy: 'It seems to us that in politics, as in life, it is more worth making a name for oneself than of trying to emerge from anonymity by judicating by fraudulently taking a name to which one is not entitled.
Liliane Bettencourt case: François-Marie Banier will be tried for abuse of weakness
Nanterre court decided on Friday to try artist François-Marie Banier in order to determine whether the daughter of the billionaire Liliane Bettencourt is accustomed, he took advantage of an octogeneous state of weakness to rob her of some of his fortune.
The 62-year-old photographer, best known for his photos of stars, will be tried on 15 and 16 April 2010 for an abuse of weakness, an offence punishable by up to three years'immprisonment and EUR 375 000.
This decision means that there will indeed be a lawsuit in this saga-already of one of the wealthiest families in France, in which mother and daughter are tearing each other apart while holding the reins of the empire of L'Oréal Cosmetics empire.
But the Correctional Chamber has also ordered an 87-year-old medical expertise from Liliane Bettencourt to be conducted collegially by three doctors.
L’Oréal’s heir and main shareholder has always refused to undergo such an examination, at least not under the conditions proposed by the Nanterre Public Prosecutor at the time of the investigation.
Physicians appointed on Friday will have to submit a report before 10 March 2010, stating in particular if Liliane Bettencourt may have been in a state of weakness and at what periods.
I think it will submit to it.
I'm going to talk to him about it, said at the end of the hearing Mrs. Bettencourt's lawyer, Georges Kiejman.
For its part, Françoise Bettencourt-Meyers' lawyer Olivier Metzner welcomed the court decision.
This is an excellent piece of news (...) Expertise will demonstrate that he (Mr Banier) has manipulated this elderly lady who must be protected, he said.
For the past two years, Liliane Bettencourt's only daughter has instituted criminal proceedings against François-Marie Banier, a photographer and artist, whom she accuses for years of taking advantage of her mother's weakness to obtain a billion euros of donations, in the form of vouchers, life insurance contracts or masterboards.
For its part, Liliane Bettencourt had always refuted having been manipulated by the photographer, whom she regarded as a long-time friend.
While the court had to consider the admissibility of Ms. Bettencourt-Meyers'feasure, the lawyers'coundories sometimes turned into a big family unbuilder on Friday.
Liliane Bettencourt's lawyer referred to three letters sent by her client (in 1999, 2003 and 2005) to her notary, in which she expressed distrust of Françoise Bettencourt-Meyers.
Liliane Bettencourt, Liliane Bettencourt added, according to the lawyer: ' Could you explain to my daughter whom I wrote to Banier once, twice a day for fifteen years.
Françoise, I've never been able to talk to her since her wedding."
For its part, the Nanterre Public Prosecutor's Office reiterated its view that the complainant was not eligible to take legal action, especially since a guardianship judge in recent days had refused in recent days to enjoin its request to open judicial protection proceedings for its mother.
Liliane Bettencourt "has full capacity to take legal action, is not under protection and has no guardian.
No one else has to act in his place,' said Marie-Chrsitine Daubigney, representative of the Public Prosecutor's Office.
The court finally decided that it would rule once the trial took place on Ms. Bettencourt-Meyers'admiss.
Managed revolution or the end of economic intelligence?
If nothing changes in the governance of big French companies, our struggle for the development of economic intelligence has been inexpensive...
For more than a decade, the attitude of the management of large companies vis-à-vis its employees reminds ourselves of the position of companies vis-à-vis consumers before the marketing revolution: it is a question of getting the employees to adhere to “values” determined from above as they used to be imposing on consumers products designed by tech technicians in the factory.
Bringing the customer at the heart of the company, the marketing revolution has put the strategies of conquesting of markets ... although the authoritarian pulsation of products disconnected from customer preference factors comes back on a regular basis.
Demobilization of employees can lead to lethal demoralization.
Susacssions at France Telecom, PSA or Renault are only the emerging part of a much more general phenomenon that particularly affects managers.
Demobilisation seriously jeopardises the development of all the intellectual functions of the company, in particular of research and development and, even more, economic intelligence.
EI “requires a proactive and forward-thought approach from all stakeholders”; it requires a commitment on the part of employees that authoritarian management recipes completely constrain.
That is why we are calling for a managerial revolution to put employees at the top of the company’s concerns on an equal footing with the customer.
The relative usefulness of internal opinion surveys
Will internal opinion surveys – such as the questionnaire submitted by Orange to its employees – incite this revolution in managerial practices?
Unfortunately, I'm afraid I won't.
First of all, because I was able to see that, in the event of a new situation, every structure finally comes, after many detours, to do as a matter of priority ... what it has always done. . . .
– Secondly, because authoritarianism – one of the pathologies of power – grows proportionally to the magnitude of its own failures.
- At the end of that, because most leaders have a simplistic view of human behaviour.
Coming from the great engineering schools, they have a mechanistic interpretation of psychology.
They adopt, out of intellectual comfort, the old behaviorist theory, recently reactivated by US “neurosciences” and widely disseminated, unbeknownst to them, by research institutes and the media.
For example, by suggesting that it is possible to manipulate “lives” (sic), a recent Ipsos investigation published in Le Figaro.fr strengthens leaders in a mechanistic governance posture unsuitable in today’s world.
Identifying and measuring mobilization factors
On the other hand, the managerial revolution necessary for the proper functioning of modern enterprises presupposes adopting a finalist vision of human behaviour.
It consists in incorporating a fundamental observation, in any reasoning or practice of governance: for employees, the commitment to the company is only possible if it clearly appears as a means of realizing part of their dreams, their ambitions and their projects.
At the present time, only studies resulting from Momentional uation adopt this conception of employee motivations.
These studies have shown that they are judging their company on the basis of a predetermined doxa that allows them, through their perceived image of the company, to evaluate what it offers them in order to carry out their life plans.
Thanks to the identification and measurement of these mobilisation factors, business leaders can control, predict and anticipate their "social performance" and return their human resources management "in place".
In this way, they create a fertile ground for the concerted development of robust and effective economic intelligence.
Speech from Oslo: "Just war" is worth Obama’s right-wing prayers
The US right on Friday hailed Barack Obama’s speech the day-or-were in Oslo, pleased that the 2009 Nobel Peace Prize laureate defended “filly war” against America’s enemies.
The Conservatives, who embroidered Mr. Obama’s award on October 9, this time applauded the presidential speech, along with Sarah Palin, a former Vice-Presidency candidate in the 2008 election.
“I liked what he said, Palin, the face of the right wing of the Republican Party, told USA Today, adding that she herself addressed the “sometime-savvy” war in her newly published memoirs.
War is, of course, the last thing in my opinion that the Americans want to do, but it is necessary.
We have to stop these terrorists right there," she said.
Mr. Obama, who inherited the two wars of his predecessor George W. Bush in Iraq and in Afghanistan, worked in Oslo on Thursday to justify the use of force, one way to explain his decision to send an additional 30,000 troops to Afghanistan nine days earlier.
Another adversary of Obama, Newt Gingrich, felt that the president had done "a very good job in Oslo".
I found that the speech was really very good, welcomed the former Speaker of the House of Representatives, who particularly liked the reference to "the existence of evil in the world" in the presidential statement.
On some other sides, I found this very historical speech, said Mr Gingrich, when asked on the radio show "The Takeaway".
The ex-chospeaker, considered by some as a possible Republican nomination candidate for the 2012 presidential election, thrilled to see "a left-wing president" reminded the members of the Nobel Committee that "they wouldn't be free and wouldn'receive a prize of peace if the use of force didn't exist".
Michael Gerson, the former feather of George W. Bush reconvening columnist in the Washington Post, Obama delivered "a very American speech".
In addition to presenting himself as a "citizen of the world" as 'he still does it', 'he has spoken like an American president', reminding Europeans "that they live in a security bubble which they have not created and do not do much to preserve it", said Mr Gerson.
Among the few unsuspecting right-wing falcon of the Bush era, former United Nations Ambassador John Bolton described the speech as an "terne, apoguided and uninspired" on the National Review Online.
To the left of the president, Democratic Representative Dennis Kucinich has alarmed that Barack Obama justifies the use of force.
When one envelops war in the notion of justice, one can easily justify the massacre of innocent people, he warned.
The centre-left press, however, remained praulous to the president.
The Los Angeles The Los Angeles Times, while continuing to believe that the Nobel was awarded prematurely to Mr. Obama, described his speech as “superproduction,” even for a speaker known for his talent.
“The Oslo Speech 'wouln't serve as a roadmap to guide international decisions against conflict, poverty and oppression,' the California-based day-to-wiwitness.
Mr. Obama "pronounced the right speech," said The New York Times, judging "both gloomy and edifying."
Drawers: three marathons for an agreement that saves Christmas Supplies
After three marathon negotiation sessions, the road trade unions and TLF, the only employers' organisation that had stayed to the very end, reached on Friday evening an agreement that puts an end to the watchword and the threat to Christmas supplies.
At the signing session on Friday evening, Secretary of State for Transport, Dominique Bussereau, "rejoiced" from this agreement, after several negotiating sittings, launched on Wednesday morning.
After a second night of discussions, the negotiators left in the early morning on Friday, with the hope of quickly signing the agreed text at an early end of the morning.
Even though three employers’ organizations (FNTR, Unostra, OTRE) shattered the burgeon.
But it still took more than six hours of a meeting started at 11 a.m. for everyone to come into agreement and the text signed at 6 p.m.
The conflict 'would have been very damaging to companies' and 'mira was afraid that it will disrupt the end-of-year holidays', said Mr Bussereau.
The text provides for an increase in wages of 4 per cent for the lowest wages (in local and regional road transport) and 2.9 per cent for the highest (international transport).
Hourly rates for low-wage wilts increase from EUR 8.72 to EUR 9.06.
It's not a big thing, but let's go a long way, explained Patrice Clos (FO).
It's going to give orders to our activists that there are no blockages, no blockades of the supply centres.
But we can still have a few recalcitrants that are unhappy, he added.
Philippe Grillot, president of TLF, welcomed "a good agreement", even though it was "difficult to conceive the increases on high wages in the light of the international competition".
In his view, the increases would be in the order of EUR 170 monthly for low-wage earners and EUR 100-120 for the highest.
This is the agreement of common-sense and respect for employees, said Maxime Dumont (CFDT), while Thierry Douine (CFTC) welcomed "a historic agreement".
Richard Jaubert (CGT), for his part, referred to "an acceptable compromise".
The agreement also provides for the upgrading of travel costs by 3 per cent, and the holding of general road transport in the first half of 2010, which will make it possible, inter alia, to address the issue of a 13th mould and the modernisation of the collective agreement.
Mr Bussereau pointed out that "a EUR 100 million reduction in employers' charges" has been the subject of a government amendment to the draft amending Finance for 2009, which was voted by the National Assembly overnight from Thursday to Friday.
The government will ask the Senate to vote "in identical terms".
The signature of TLF makes it possible that the agreement may initially apply to undertakings which are members of this organisation, and secondly, to the whole profession, in the event of an extension by the Minister of Labour.
It will be "as soon as possible", assured Mr Bussereau.
Opposite employers' organisations can only delay the process.
The FNTR denounced a 'duped market', as the EUR 100 million "had already been pledged to the sector to offset part of the carbon tax".
The Secretary of State for Transport forced us to negotiate in an urgent manner, not at the end of a shared analysis, but in order not to upset turkey eaters from the next New Year's Eve.
For the Otre, there is a risk that the agreement is 'dreader of a very large number of SMEs in the sector over the next 12 months'.
RER A: Disruptions this weekend, trade unions dissatisfied with RATP proposals
Disruptions on RER A, France's largest urban public transport line, will continue over the weekend, while a meeting on the 2nd day of strike between trade unions and RATP leadership left them hungry for their demand for a premium.
