A new meeting is scheduled for "a broader review of the conditions for practising the profession", RATP said.
This also made the trade unions say that the company 'mocked the users of the weekend.
By the morning, some 150 drivers invaded an establishment committee at headquarters in Paris.
It was then that the management, which had hitherto refused any discussion during the strike, had suggested the resemblance of "appeasement".
The conflict is looming towards the RER B on Tuesday, following the submission of notice by Unsa and Independents.
The trade unions are asking for a bonus of EUR 120 (Unsa) to EUR 150, of which EUR 30 varies (CGT, FO, CFDT, South, Self-Employed) and are complaining about the deterioration in working conditions on this line per million passengers per day.
RER drivers, both in the second part of their quarantine, earn from EUR 2,200 to 2,700 gross and EUR 600 to EUR 650 for staggered hours on Sundays and public holidays.
The Turkish judiciary disobeminates the pro-Kurdish party, a decision fringes
The Turkish Constitutional Court on Friday handed out the dissolution of the country's main pro-Kurdish party, a decision which was immediately followed by burgeon demonstrations in the Kurdish community, in the south-east of the country and in Istanbul.
This decision risks greatly complicating the task of the government, which has just extended its hand to the Kurdish community in an attempt to put an end to 25 years of conflict.
The Party for a Democratic Society (DTP) was dissipated because it had become a "center of activities prejudicial to the independence of the State and its indivisable unity", Court President Hasim Kiliç told the press after four days of deliberation.
The decision had been taken unanimously by the 11 judges, while a seven-vote qualified majority had been required to dissolve, said Mr Kiliç, adding that 37 of the party's executives, including its President Ahmet Tork and MP Aysel Tugluk, were banned from political life for a period of five years.
The judge also announced the waiver of the parliamentary immunity enjoyed by Mr. Tork and Ms. Tugluk and the forfeiture by the treasury of party property.
The DTP has 21 seats in Parliament (out of 550).
Its leaders had warned before the verdict that MPs would leave Parliament rather than sit as parliamentarians unlicensed.
The decision follows a procedure initiated in 2007 by the Prosecutor of the Court of Cassation Abdurrahman Yalçinkaya, accusing DTP of obeying the "guidelines" of the Kurdistan Workers' Party (PKK), regarded as a terrorist organization by Ankara and in many countries.
Many of the observers believe that the DTP is the legal political showcase of the PKK Resales.
However, the party claimed that it had "no organic ties" with the PKK but refused to call it a terrorist organisation and called on the government to negotiate with it.
The verdict of the Court comes at a time when the government has just extended its hand to the Kurdish community by proposing a series of measures to reinforce its rights, in an attempt to decelebrate Kurds’ support for the PKK and to put an end to the conflict.
DTP leaders had claimed that dissolution could lead to an increase in tensions in south-eastern Anatolian, where numerous protests against the conditions of detention of PKK leader Abdullah Ocalan have degenerated clashes with the police in recent weeks.
Upon the announcement of the courts, a thousand demonstrators gathered in front of the DTP premises in Diyarbakir, the largest city in south-east populated by Kurds.
Law enforcement officials threw tear-producing grenades and used water cannons, while demonstrators threw molotov cocktails or stones with sydring.
About 100 people also demonstrated in Istanbul.
After the verdict, he stated that the decision of the judiciary would "explore desperation".
Turkey could not resolve this issue by banning a party, he told the press.
The government, made up by an Islamist party, also lamented the decision.
As a matter of principle, we are opposed to the closure of the parties.
It doesn't solve anything, said Energy Minister Taner Yildiz.
This decision is a total torpedo torpedo of the democratic openness launched by the government, commented political analyst Ahmet Insel.
We can, of course, expect a PKK reaction, he added.
On Thursday, the PKK boasted an attack that had killed seven soldiers three days earlier in northern Turkey, one of the deadliest in recent months.
Early snows can fall
What Wolfgang Brauburger wants is a very harsh winter.
However, the person in charge of Road Clearance and Sand-blasting (FES) points out that this is not annoying the citizens.
Simply so that the agents can test the technique of vehicles and learn to master them well.
And according to meteorologists, it may well be for this weekend.
More than 300 staff and 120 vehicles stand ready to tackle snow and ice.
The FES here follows a very elaborate plan.
Mr Brauburger explained that, as soon as it rushed into the city of Frankfurt and when the temperatures fell below zero, large sand-blasting vehicles travel 20 main itineraries (referred to as Routes A).
This includes high-traffic streets, the main arteries, such as Neue Weg and Hanauer Landstrasse.
It also includes fire access, public transport itineraries, pedestrian areas and sloping streets such as the Atzelbergstrai.
FES officers begin their service at three o'clock in the morning.
If necessary, the 10-40-kilometers of roads must be saturated with a lorry melter that adheres well, within a maximum of 3 working hours.
This is why the FES service starts at 3:00 a.m. "so that the roads will be cleared when daily migration traffic begins".
Where it gets more difficult, it's when the snow starts to fall at 3:30 a.m.
This is a matter of problems, the person in charge of the clearing and stowing service of the roads 'for no motorist is willing to let the salting vehicles pass.
Then, if it is no longer snowing, access roads and utility roads - such as the Rat-Beil-Straye.
Side lanes and side streets come last.
Mr Brauburger is well aware that this always leads to a number of complaints.
At five o'clock in the morning, the FES hiking team set out to identify as a matter of priority the areas deemed to be undangerous.
Cycling ladders, the services of FES are generally unable to deal with them.
Mr. Brauburger pointed out that there was a special service in Frankfurt which was responsible for overseeing the condition of decks between 22:00 a.m.
As ice forms very rapidly on these surfaces, salting vehicles come into play as soon as the temperature drops below 2 degrees.
It is also possible for the police to make use of this special service in need.
The cradle of dinosaurs
The Tawa Hallae resembled other carnivorous dinosaurs: this biped was about 2 meters long for 1.50 meters high, its hijackers were powerful, its elongated skull was provided with shredded and curved teeth, its short arms fleas ended with sickle clutches and it had a long tail.
If this species had lived a few million years later, it wouldn't have had anything exceptional.
But, as Sertling Nesbitt from the University of Texas in Austin reports and his colleagues in Thursday's Science Magazine, Tawa Hallae is one of the first dinosaurs with these morphological features.
This animal lived 215 million years ago in New Mexico, which at the same time became part of supercontinent's land, Pangaea, and was towards the equator.
Sertling Nesbitt says the discovery of Tawa blooms the evolution of early dinosaurs from a new day.
Indeed, it informs us about kinship relations, distribution and their characteristics.
It was in 2006 that Sertling Nesbitt undertook excavations with other paletontologists on the Gohst Ranch site in northern New Mexico
Randall Irmis of the Natural Story Museum of Utah and member of the excavation team reshearts that they sometimes found a dozen ostensions in one day which is absolutely unique for a sedimentary dating from the Triates.
Scissors soon realized that some of these bones belonged to a new species.
Excavations uncovered the remains of five specimens.
A discovery that confirms the theories regarding the early evolution of dinosaurs.
The researchers named it from the Tawa name according to the Indian Hopi Word for the Sun's god.
For Sterling Nesbitt, this is quite an exceptional discovery: dinosaur fossils derived from the Trias are extremely rare and are limited to a few incomplete bone fragments.
Not all palaeontological experts share this enthusiasm.
For example Martin Sander of the University of Bonn did not find that this discovery was particularly spectacular, but he pointed out that it could confirm certain theories regarding the early evolution of dinosaurs.
According to the current state of knowledge, dinosaurs appeared on the Earth about 230 million years ago.
However, most of the older fossils are fragmented and incompleted, making it very difficult to classify the earliest dinosaurs.
Among other things, experts are wondering when saurians were divided into three major evolutionary groups: the theropod (carnivores) that include the tyrannosaurus and velociraptor that appeared later, from which birds, sauropods, quadrupeds long-necked, and those related to them and herbivores ornithischiens of which species such as species such.
According to studies by S. Nesbitt and his colleagues, Tawa is one of the first theropods.
This species exhibits an interesting ratio of both primitive and developed characteristics, called S. Nesbitt.
Researchers found clues to suggest that Tawa lobbies displayed small aerial sacks in its skull bones, just like birds descended from the theropods.
These small pockets of air facilitate the respiration of birds compared to mammals.
It appears that in the Tawa Hallae the vertebbrae were also partially air-filled.
According to Randall Irmis, the Tawa is one of the oldest theropods featuring small aerial sacs in its bones.
As the related line between the immense sauropods possessed aerial sacks and light skelevolving skelevolts, one might think that their common ancestor already had this beneficial innovation.
Three successive waves of migrating to North America.
As the most well-known prinosaurs lived in South America, the researchers concluded that saurians had appeared there.
As a result, the three major evollement lines were divided from South America to the rest of the world, i.e. all of the land-emerged lands that formed the supercontinent Pangaea.
This seems to be confirmed by the fact that paleontologists have also uncovered the remains of two other theropods that are not particularly related.
The three species cannot descend from a common ancestor that would have migrated, the researchers argue.
Alan Turner of the Museum of Natural History in New York and co-leader of the magazine Science Studies said that there had been at least three waves of migration to North America.
Of course, there are some contradictions.
Martin Sander, palaeontologist in Bonn, says that there is an even oldest species, silesaur, which lived in present-day Poland and belongs to a dinosaur-related group.
According to him, this could give a mistaken view, because in South America there is a large number of dinosaur fossils that have been preserved, while there are practically none on other continents.
On the other hand, how dinosaurs managed to become the dominant tetrapods of the secondary era when they originated from a relatively rare collateral line of the reptile branch still remains a mystery.
At the time of the triassic, dinosaurs shared their habitat with many other reptiles, such as the ancestors of current crocodiles and with many other saurians.
Towards the end of the triassicur dinosaurs spread throughout the planet, thus promoting the development of many new species, while others, which were related to them, disappeared between the Triassic and the Jurassic.
Such success, however, is not eternal.
To explain this transformation, Martin Sander saw two hypotheses.
There may have been mass ectrolean extinction that the dinosaurs survived randomly while other reptiles would have disappeared.
Either dinosaurs were more suitable and they were therefore able to eliminate competition on the ground.
In any case, such success is not eternal.
150 Million Years later, the fall of favourite meteorite development by a group of animals which had also developed during the Trias and which since that time had been lying in the shade: magnificent.
Solidarity from Vienna to Sao Paulo
