Project S21: a brake for Stuttgart
The breadth of the Stuttgart 21 project cannot be high enough.
For its followers it is nothing less than “New Heart of Europe.”
S21 is much more than the burial of a railway station, more than a colossal construction project that will plunge the heart of the capital of Baden-Wurttemberg into a desperate chaos of yards for a decade.
Stuttgart 21 originated in the spirit of the 1980s.
Lead-of-the-river stations, with lanes ending in the mid-city, had the reputation at the time of relegations of 19th-century transport technology, which were the shoe-shunting of modern rail traffic because it once needed a lot of time to pass the locomotives from the rear to the fore.
Arrival, short judgment, departure – that is what we have to do.
This is why head-of-the-line stations must disappear: in Frankfurt and in Munich.
And in Stuttgart as well: a train station, which is about halfway on the Trans-European Magistral of Rail between Paris and Budapest/Stahuta, hence the slogan 'Heart of Europe'.
The implementation of the S21 project will really begin, although the visions of the '80s no longer been compatible with reality for a long time.
Even the most ambitious planners acknowledged that the cost of burying the station in the respective capitals of Hessen and Bavaria was exorbitant.
In fact, head-of-the-line stations no longer pose real problems of entry and exit since the existence of modern, reversible trains with railcars and pilot cars at both extremities.
However, Stuttgart 21 is now launched next to the new route from the inter-city line to Ulm.
Regional and communal policies have prevailed.
The railway company is part of it because it is the taxpayer that bears the risks of the largest infrastructure project in the Federal Republic and not the public company.
From an urbanistic point of view, this project is relatively well justified.
We get an urban space that – according to computer-generated planner graphics – will be very green.
For the rail transport system the S21 is harmful.
The crossing station and enlarging of the section are designed for traffic of people to be attributed – and this is typical of the 1980s – a huge increase.
At the time there was still competition from low-cost flights.
Modern traffic policy should focus first and foremost on increasing freight traffic, because rail transport is much more environmentally friendly than by truck on the road.
But the new layout, with its inclinations, is not suitable for freight trains.
The S21, and this is much more serious indirectly affecting many other railway projects.
For example, EUR six billion will be swallowed up in the Swabi project.
This is the official figure.
Some experts believe that 10 to 15 billion are more realistic.
One thing is certain, in the next decade this money will miss other junction and congestion points in the network.
The Ministry of Transport list mentions a total of 60 major projects.
One of the most important is the expansion of the section through the Rhine valley by 180 good kilometers.
Since 1987 this line has been built in slow motion.
It would really be urgent to press the mushroom here thoroughly.
But the S21 project has the effect of a brake shoe.
The itinerary of the Rhine Valley is on the verge of international freight traffic, right in the heart of Europe.
