Dream Worlds
Some people see, awake, images and hear voices that no one else sees or hears.
It is not only psychic patients, or under the effect of drugs, that the boundaries between imagination and reality merge.
As long as these phenomena occur during sleep, everything goes very well.
When lying in our dun Submitted rooms, we can indulge unobviatedly indulging in the wildest dreams and fantasies.
However, he who, awake, experiences a state of a dream, hears and sees things that others do not perceive, quickly has the reputation in our Western world of being mad or drugged, at the very least of being an original.
Such images and vocals do not appear only in psychic patients or under the influence of drugs.
Our imaginative is at stake: it is the same neural activity that allows us to conceivate houses, paint paintings or write novels, but which sometimes inspires the boundaries between dream, madness and reality to melting.
Conscious awakening, taking the form of a dream, can present itself in the most diverse forms, in the best of cases we call visions, at the worst of madness.
But even if the trigger and the effect stand out, the visions of mysthetic Hildegard von Bingen, the drug experiences of the New Age Prophet Carlos Castaneda, shman’s supporting states, psychoses and hallucinations are attributable to very similar processes.
Hallucinations are hallucinations or delusions when the balance, neurotransmitting substances in the brain – first and foremost dopamine, noradrenaline, serotonin and achétyl choline – breaks and the interaction between the different parts of the brain is disturbed.
Complex hallucinations and delustrious thoughts very often appear during schizophrenic psychosis.
Terrestrial attraction no longer has power over the person.
Since antiquity, Philosophers and Physicians have always investigated the relationship between madness and Dream.
For Aristotle, hallucinations were a form of awakened dream; Wilhelm Griesinger, one of the founders of modern psychiatry, observed in 1861 "a great resemblance between madness and the dream", for Sigmund Freud the dream was even "a psychosis with all irregularities, imaginaries, delusions and sensual delusions that compose it".
It's Autumn 2001 when Henning T turns into a comic book.
Its body is in two dimensions with loving colors.
Earth attraction no longer has any power over it, it hovers over the ceiling.
He feels a blow.
Behind him winged devil creatures, grinding, with a bloody mouth, showing the teeth.
They chase him, kick him with heavy flamboyant boots, threw him against the walls, he felt pain throughout his body.
Throthy, ugly, thrilling, scream, coming out of the walls and the radio.
It can no longer withstood those voices that insult and humiliate it.
Henning T. bleeds two cables from its stereo chain, actually a buckle and attaches it to a hook attached to the celebration.
It climbs onto a baffle and passes the buckle around its neck.
He's jumping.
The buckle becomes detached.
Let this be a lesson, scream the voices in a chorus to the eardrum.
Today Henning T. is 40 years old, he has experienced six acute psychoses, the last of 2 years, the first of almost 20.
It was triggered by LSD.
Diagnosis: schizo-affective, paranoid psychosis, in addition it is manic-depressive, it takes medicines against depression, mania, schizophrenia.
He learned to live with his illness, he has been married for three years.
During the psychosis I experienced terrible fears and deep despairs.
Illusions are a cruel reality.
About one per cent of the population suffers at least once in its life from schizophrenia; in psychiatric clinics it is the most common post-depression disease.
In a crisis of schizophrenia, the people concerned can no longer distinguish between the inner world and the outside world, they find themselves in a state of hyper-excitation, which means that they may suffer from hallucinations, megalomania, relational madness or persecution.
It is very fluctuous to hear voices – voices that control, comment, or dialogue.
Optical hallucinations are not uncommon either.
The person concerned can no longer make a distinction between what is important and the unsprinkler, the brain can no longer filter the disruptive signals, the conscious is drifted under the sensation.
Just like drave, psychosis opens the locks to a tide of ideas and fantasies from the deeper strategies of consciousness.
Perceivation of the reality in psychosis is focused on the subject, according to Professor Thomas Fuchs, Head of the Department of psychiatry at Heidelberg Hospital (Allgemeine Psychiatry im Universitettesklinikum).
The schizophrenic is at the heart of the situation, all with a meaning focused on it.
At the same time it ends up in a passive role, it is like a dream, overcome by events and cannot act actively on them.
This is why schizophrenics often feel threatened, controlled or manipulated.
Usually they don’t have the ability to adjust the reality, delusions are a cruel reality, waking up is excluded.
To experience a hallucination once in one's life.
Unreasonably, according to Fuchs, "the sensory and spatial structure of reality that is perceived during psychosis is fundamentally conserved."
Unrelating thoughts and hallucinations are based on reality, the alienation of experience takes place in a real framework.
Why does our brain make this kind of cabriole is always an enigma for researchers?
Unfortunate situations can cause everyone to temporarily lose the perception of reality, according to Fuchs.
But only a small number of people will develop psychosis.
There seems to be a genetic predisposition to schizophrenia, but twin studies have shown that genes aren't the only determining factors.
The researchers assume that the people concerned have minimal brain damage.
Neural maturing in the brain is assumed to be disrupted at an early stage of development, e.g. viral infection of the mother during pregnancy, explains Fuchs.
But what remains unexplained is the way the anomaly of the neneurural system occurs, and the fact that it doesn't manifest itself for many years.
Environmental factors also play a role – Stress or turbulent experience can be a trigger of psychosis, if there is predisposal.
Similarly hallucinogenic drugs can trigger psychoses because they act on neurotransmitters.
When our brain heats up.
What's mad, Psychological Professor Erich Kasten of Labeck tells us is that psychotics suffer from their hallucinations and take expensive drugs to get rid of them, while others buy very expensive drugs to get them.
Pleasant or unpleasant perception of delusions is often just a matter of perspective.
Over the past ten years, Kasten has collected reports on hallucinations and illusory image phenomena: last year he published his results in a book entitled 'The Unreal World in Our Head.
Hallucinations are indeed among the concomitant phenomena typical of psychoses but also of an impressive number of many other pathologies.
These include psychic disorders such as borderline or depression, but also migraine, epilepsy, multiple sclerosis, Alzheimer’s disease or head trauma.
Surprising is that under certain conditions even the brains of healthy people are prone to hallucinations.
The hallucinations, according to Kasten, consist of fragments of memorabilia that are normally inhibited.
They take shape when the areas of the brain that treat sensory perceptions, are hyperactive or not sufficiently restrained; when our brain heats but also when there's a lack of stimulation.
Drugs can unravel this state just like brain injury or psychosis.
But also the lack of companions, the stress, the chagrins of loving or other exceptional mental states.
In recent studies, one-third of the participants acknowledge having experienced hallucination at least once in their lives.
In the summer of 2005 Sarah K. is 16 years old, she lives in a small town of Mecklenburg-Vorpommer and goes to high school.
Girl who really needs to be normal.
If it wasn't pictures and voices.
During the re-ict, it happens that a young boy, slender with long chestnut-haired hair, speak to him.
When she answered his comrades, looked at it, astonished – no one else sees or hears this boy.
They think this shy girl is a lunatic dreamer, but Sarah K doesn't dream, she's sure.
She is waking, hearing and seeing this boy just as she sees and hears the girl sitting beside her on the bench.
It can even perceive its presence momentarily.
It's a pleasant feeling, she finds this youngster sympathetic.
"I am insane?" she sometimes wonders.
But she is well aware that this young person and the other visions are not real and that they will disappear again.
Nursery apprisings, in general, frightening.
Frequent visions are heavy.
When she's in the classroom, sometimes she can't take the course because the teacher is just presenting a new student in his eyes and ears while he's invisible to the others and the teacher is visibly expedating equations on the board.
Nursery apprisings, in general, frightening.
When, in the obscurity of her bedroom, she waits for sleep that comes too rarely and far too late, she often cannot make a distinction if she is awake or dream already, if the images she sees, greedy faces or the young girl with bloody notches on her arms and leg are in front of her eyes open or closed.
Leave me quiet, scream at these creatures.
She didn't talk to his parents or her friends about it; they would take her for a madwoman.
Autumn in 2009. Sarah K. is now 20 years old.
She had her baccalaureate last year and she lives with her friend.
Hallucinations ceased a year a year ago.
Since coming into contact via the Internet with the psychologist Kasten she knows very well that she is not insane.
Madness is also a work of art of desperation.
When someone is socially insuspected or does not have sufficient stimulation, there is a high probability that they will have hallucinations after a certain period of time.
I guess the brain wasn't busy enough, makes sure that there's animation.
Sarah K., the only daughter, was scared of the other children when she was a kid and often played alone.
Her hallucinations began when she learned at the age of 13 that the man she called daddy, since she was of an estimated age, was not her real father.
These are the same cerebral areas that are active for visual and acoustic hallucinations, and for actual impressions and noises, it is therefore very difficult for people like Sarah K to make the difference between delusion and reality.
Hallucinations are also images that form in the brain, which is why they seem so real."
For Kasten, Night Dreams are also a form of hallucination.
The hallucinations of healthy people do not differ fundamentally from those in acute psychosis.
Unlike schizophrenics, the mentally healthy person can recognize that fantasies aren't real pictures.
This is why Kasten advises his patients to cherish hallucinations as a particular talent.
There is only one step between dream and hallucination.
In our world too focused on consciousness we unfortunately quickly tend to take imaginary experiences for pathologies, according to Michael Schmidt-Degenhard, Professor in Heidelberg and Chief Medical Officer of psychiatry Clinic and Psycho Therapy at Florence Nightingale Hospital in Dusseldorf.
A psychosis is a serious, painful disease, but that also contains something positive, a creator – madness is an artwork of desperation.
Schmidt-Degenhard studied the so-called Oniroid experience, a particular form of the modification of consciousness awakened by the dream.
Uniroid experiences are very complex semi-dreams which are perceived as real by those who live them and which they cannot differentiate from the dream even when it is in a state of waking.
Oniroid consciousness is manifested during trauma or in situations of extreme conditions, such as patients with polyradiculopathy who are fully aware, are experiencing motor paralysis.
According to Schmidt-Degenhard, when a human being is threatened by a loss of oneself and the world, due to a total and long-lasting inability to move, it replaces this real situation that harms their lives by its own imaginary world that is dissociated from reality.
It is a state of mental boundary between dream and hallucination.
As it is not possible to overcome the situation in reality, a global change is taking place, but the real situation is represented in imaginative.
It is, in fact, an intense form of means and interpretation, in a situation that is intolerable in itself, an organisational capacity that is given to us, men, so as not to slide into the lack of loss of consciousness.
Everything is real even later in memories.
Peter Cornelius Classen, an art historian also describes his oniroid experiences in his book "Changation of the Heart" as an action to safeguard the soul.
He's 49 years old, at a time when his conscience leaves for a journey of several days after the operation of a heart valve followed by a transplant; during this time, his body is doomed to remain immobile resuscitating.
Claussen immersed himself in a world that he created from scratch, visiting foreign places, traveling in time.
In the Middle Ages, it lives among nobles who produce blood-based narcotics.
He is kidnapped by a motorbike who takes him to his country, surfing his hospital bed in the Alpine Breath and meets a Korean Mafia.
It is completely absorbed in this imaginary world, everything is real even later in its memories.
According to Claussen, domestic journeys are fundamentally distinguished from all dreams.
Dream is a completely different state, he writes, describing what it went through.
Contrary to dreams, the memories coming from mental travel are extremely clear and very detailed even years later.
It can even be said that they are much more distinct and more intensive than real experience.
The intensity of this experience erupts the psychiatrist Schmidt-Degenhard from the psychiatrist.
In the end, airid delusions, psychoses and psychedelic psychedelic delusions show us how fragile our inner representations of the external reality even in a state of waking.
Any change in the state of consciousness, psychologist Kasten "may cause a distortion of what we call reality."
An experiment that can also be quite enriching.
