zapatero sees “a future of tranquillity” for the engine sector
The President of the Government, José Luis Rodraguez zapatero, sees “a future of tranquillity” for the Spanish automotive industry, which, he recalled, is the country’s main export sector.
At the presentation of the Government’s Economic Report for 2009, zapatro recalled the measures launched by the executive during the year to support the automotive sector.
“Without an Automotive Sector Competitiveness Plan, today we would have a landscape of abandonment and closure of some of our main production sites,” said the President of the Government.
“Thanks to this plan and the decision of brands to stay in Spain, in a very competitive sector, we can envisage a future of tranquillity for the future of this industry, which is our main export sector,” he added.
In doing so, he pointed out that the Competitiveness Plan, included in the Integrated Plan for the Automotive Sector (PIA), devoted EUR 800 million to the projects of 19 manufacturers and 137 sub-companied undertakings, which mobilised an investment of more than EUR 3 000 million.
The Chief Executive explained that this investment was intended both for the development of new models and for the modernisation of existing products and processes.
It has also focused on government-to-receivable support measures, alluding to Plan 2000E, implemented in collaboration with the Autonomous Communities and Automotive Enterprises.
According to the President, this plan reduced by 6 per cent the average emissions of new vehicles marketed on the Spanish market and contributed to a “strong increase” in registrations, which increased by “40 per cent” in November.
