75 per 100 per cent of working mothers had professional problems as a result of their motherhood
Discrimination at work has increased in recent years, especially for women from younger generations who want to be mothers.
This is revealed by the study “Fertility and professional career for women in Spain”, carried out by CSIC in collaboration with the Instituto de la Mujer.
The survey of 9,737 women between the ages of 15 and 75 leaves no room for doubt: 75 per cent of working mothers had their professional careers limited because of motherhood.
The study indicates that there are many differences regarding the onset of coupy life and the number of children, whether or not the woman is working and the type of job held.
Those that do not work outside the home, work part-time or have an unsuspotential job, start a couple earlier, have their first pregnancy earlier and have more children.
On the other hand, women in stable employment, mainly in the public sector, repulsive co-exercising life and first pregnancy, and have fewer children.
Between the ages of 35 and 49 with fixed-settlement jobs, on an average of 3.7 years after settling with a couple, rising to 4.1 among those working in the public sector. “These data show that women in stable employment have needed more time in their lives to obtain it, which has led them to repulse the union and the arrival of children.
In addition, the type of employment exerted largely influences motherhood, but less the starting date of cohabitation,” explains CSIC Investigator Margarita Delgado.
The level of education is another factor that widens the gap in the marriage age of women and their first child.
According to the survey, the higher the level of training, the more they get married and have their first child at a later stage.
The difference is also maintained among women of the same generation.
For example, in groups aged between 35 and 49, those with elementary and higher education, for the first time, were mothers for the first time at an average of 25.1 and 32.1 years, respectively.
“Another disctinction also appears between women, depending on their level of education.
While those who have completed elementary studies marry and have their first child before finding a stable job, those who have studied at the middle or higher level reverse this programme and give priority to obtaining stable work as a prerequisite for marriage and the arrival of a child,” says Delgado.
