How much should you be paid according to your Facebook Profession?
Users of Facebook can know the salary they deserve according to their profile, thanks to a new application of Trovit, the search engine with the largest number of real-estate advertisements, jobs and vehicles in Spain.
The “How much should you be paid?” tool calculates users’ salaries based on more than 140,000 job openings for Trovit available in Spain, based on a form available on Facebook, where you have to register your profession, your years of experience, your age, and the city where you want to work.
For the sake of the application, the search engine has detected that, as a general rule, the Spanish worker “feels ill-paid,” Trovit Product Manager Albert Ribera said in a statement.
According to the data available to Trovità from its search engines in the United Kingdom and France, Spaniards are the lowest.
A British computer programmer receives EUR 45,226 per year gross and a EUR 31 059, while a British programmer receives EUR 24 000.
By the same to the same way, a driver receives EUR 34,247 gross annually in England, EUR 25 751 in France and EUR 16 420 in Spain.
According to Trovit, the lowest-frildened work in Spain is that of a wait-and-for-server, with EUR 16,592 net annually, followed by that of seller, with EUR 14,725, and that of a worker with EUR 15,667.
The most sought-after jobs on the Internet in the last three months have been driver, part-time jobs and social workers.
Brazil sets a starting date for zellaya
The Brazilian Government has set a deadline for the Stay of Manuel zellaya in his Embassy in Tegucigalpa.
The inverted president must abandon the diplomatic legation no later than 27 January 2010, the date of the official end of its term of office.
Francisco Catunda, an inactivity officer of the Brazilian embassy in the Honduran’s capital, told TV Globo that “Mel” knows he must leave the embassy, where he has been a refugee since September 21, before that date.
“He is aware that he must depart on 27 January, at the end of his mandate, he must look for another destination”.
The channel also quoted the former pruner officer, who confirmed his intention to leave the embassy before the end of the established deadline: “My position is to leave as soon as possible, logically with the support of the Brazilian government,” zelaya said by telephone.
After the elections of 29 November (of which Porfirio Lobo had been elected), he assured that he would remain in the Brazilian embassy until January.
However, in the same week, he tried to seek refuge in Brazil.
Eventually, the operation has failed, as it has refused the condition of political exile imposed on it by Roberto Micheletti.
