Once again, it seems to be an excellent opportunity to buy Spanish assets, even if for the time being “precaution is a safe mother”.
Connected sectors
Iberdrola’s president, Ignacio Sanchez Galon, was the first to address the subcommittee of the congregation of Deputies yesterday, where Spain’s energy strategy for the next two decades is being discussed these days.
After his intervention, which was followed by that of the president of Gas Natural-Fenosa, Rafael Villaseca, Mr Galon sketched out for the press what he had mentioned before the sub-committee, at an eight-closed.
The President of Iberdrola, Spain needs to invest EUR 50 000 million by 2020 and EUR 60 000 more between this date and 2030, should nuclear power plants currently in operation remain open.
If that is not the case, the necessary investments would not amount to EUR 60 000 million but to EUR 95 000.
On the other hand, Iberdola believes that for the next decade, investment should not be devoted to increasing production capacity, but to distribution networks and international interconnections.
Beginning in 2020, the issue has changed, and power will need to be increased.
Its gamble focuses on both gas-fired and coal-fired power, carbon-democracy-revelocating mechanisms and, above all, on renewable energies.
In this area, it sees an additional 18,000 MW needed, together with other “supporting energies”.
Galon, who argues that the draft Sustainable Economy Law that the government has just approved is in line with what its company is demanding, did not wish to attack the royal decree aimed at encouraging national coal consumption, which contradicts anti-global-moderirm policies.
According to him, there is “a clear long-term bet” in this direction, despite the “off-one measures,” as Iberdrola’s president called them.
In any case, Galon believes that decisions to invest, even though long-term, must begin to be taken, because it takes some time to reach maturity and that a stable earning framework is needed to address them.
Ferrovial reaches Aer Lingus Handshandling Market at 10 airports
The Swiss company Swissport, Ferrovial, a subsidiary of Ferrovial for airport services on land, will act as privileged partner of the Irish airline Aer Lingus for freight services at ten European airports in which it is present.
