CNMV assured that it had not been informed of this offer, although Ruiz Mateos had stated that it had contacted the regulator.
If it took place, payment would amount to 611.7 million, compared with Sos’s capital of USD 254.27 million.
In the meantime, Sos continues the restructuring of its management.
In an attempt to remain outside of possible offers of purchases and the judicial comeback of its former directors, the brothers Jesus and Jaime Salazar, she announced the election of Maria Luis Jord as internal audit director.
A decision that makes it the first woman to hold a leading position in the food company.
It is a newly created post which will depend directly on the chairman of the group.
And it is not policipable, since the company remains submerged in the legal battle for the alleged diversion of more than 230 million Sos to Salazar companies.
Moreover, the possibility that in the coming months Sos may reveal new embezzlement of funds cannot be set aside.
With regard to credit to the Salazar, former Councillors Ildefonso Ortega (representatives of the CCM and Unicaja) told the National Exami (representatives of the MLCBI) told the National Audience yesterday that they had been informed of the misbehaviour of EUR 212 without knowing its destination.
CNMV Authorizes Paternina Proliferation Take-Off by Inversora
The National Securities Markets Commission (CNMV) yesterday approved Inversora Sea’s take-off on Federico Paternina on 9 October.
The monitoring body explained that it authorized this operation because it understood that its terms adjusted to the standards in force and that it considered “sufficient” the content of the Explanatory Sheet submitted, following the changes applied on 2 December.
The CNMV clarified that the offer concerns the acquisition of 550,008 shares of Federico Paternina at EUR 7.65 per share, representing 8.955% of the share capital, consisting of 6,214,786 shares accepted for negotiation on the Madrid and Bilbao stock exchanges.
It further explained that 5,592,778 million shares were immobilised and could in no way be transmitted prior to the finalisation of the tender, the price of which he stated had been fixed by Federico Paternina, in accordance with the provisions of the standards in force.
New Tools for Car Expertise
In these days of bad weather and accidents, car garages are not the only winners.
Every day, thrifting cars are brought back to a garage where an expert must assess the damage suffered and the cost of the repair.
Each loss is different, and the calculation of the cost of coins and labour time can be a long and complex job.
Calculate by hand the cost of a repair between EUR 20 000 and EUR 30 000, including coins and labour, may take two or three hours.
On the other hand, with the assistance of the most up-to-date computer tools, this would be a matter of minutes.
It is for this reason that almost 100 per 100 per cent of experts make use of such systems.
Suppliers for loss assessment and maintenance are dedicated to the collection of information provided by the manufacturers and then to process it and offer it to their customers in the form of a useful tool.
In Spain, Audatex of the Nord-American Solera group is the leading company; 80 per cent of estimates are made with its products.
The other companies competing in the field of evaluation tools and the development of information on the sector are GtMotive and Eurotax.
The work with the data provided by the manufacturers is far from simple.
According to Eduardo Velzquez, commercial director of Audatex, the company in Spain with 63 manufacturers and importers, and each of them provides them with information in a different format.
In addition, the amount of data to be processed is constantly increasing, so over the last five years, more versions, variants and engines have been launched than over the previous 15 years.
When a new car comes out on the market, these companies get to work.
This is a very arduous job, because it is necessary to catalog each of the thousand parts of the vehicle and record its price.
In addition, an “smart” graphic image of each model can be clicked on the different parts of a passenger car.
If it is a model sold heavily, the work can be ready within a week if the process progresses very quickly.
In the case of low-selling cars, it can take three or four months. Audatex currently has complete information on 99.2% of cars marketed in Spain.
Among the most exclusive models, they have, for example, information about the Porsches, but not the Ferrari or the Lamborghini.
With regard to vehicles in circulation, the figure exceeds 99%.
To carry out documentation and database development, Audatex has eight centres around the world (in Spain, France, Germany, the United States, the United States, Brazil, Mexico, China and Japan), each of which specializes in selected brands.
The national headquarters is located in Alcobendas (Madrid).
Here, more than a hundred technicians (usually mechanic experts) work simultaneously on three computer monitors.
Audatex invests EUR 90 million each year to develop these databases.
Every month, 2.5 million data are recorded, and customers receive updates every two days.
The development of this huge flow of information allows these companies to give them different uses and to offer a wide variety of products.
The most frequent application concerns repairs and maintenance, but depending on the country, there are also tools for the management of breaking parts or for calculating the fair or residual value of a car.
The only company that has a guide that provides information on the value of second-hand cars in Spain is Eurotax.
Audatex doesn't offer it in our own country, but it does in the United States.
Given the products offered by Audatex, it is easy to get an idea of what kind of support it can provide to professionals in the sector.
This company offers ten different tools, the flagship product being AudaPlus, the standard disaster assessment solution aimed at experts.
It includes cars, motorbikes, light and heavy industrial vehicles.
In addition to the cost of parts, it shall take into account the repair times estimated by the manufacturer or the painting scales.
This tool is bought according to its use, for each estimate.
The highest cost is EUR 5.75, for customers who make little use, up to a maximum of ten times a month.
Secondly, each insurance company, which carries out thrifting thousands, negotiates its price with Audatex.
Other products, such as AudaVIN, allow the total identification of a vehicle and of the equipment with which it left the assembly line, only thanks to its chassis number.
AudaGlass focuses on vehicle loungers, and AudaSubastas is an Internet-enabled professional server that makes it possible to bid on vehicles declared wreck.
Its statistic service is very useful for identifying trends, performing geo-demographic analyses or experiencing common problems by brand or model.
It is possible to study, for example, how many models of a specified brand have gone to the garage for a breakdown or how much serious disasters have suffered for a given period.
Gt Motive is the only Spanish company dedicated to the development of such tools.
Among other things, Gt Estimate, which is also intended for the experts and supplies digital images, as well as access to the official parts, prices and time of the manufacturer.
Gt Motive is an Einsa group company, founded in 1971, as a provider of services and solutions for the assessment of claims, breakdowns or car maintenance.
The Assessment WG guide is a historical reference product on the market.
The company works with 24,000 garage users, 3,100 experts and 53 insurance companies.
Its turnover has increased at an annual rate of 20% over the last three years, accounting for EUR 10.8 million in 2008.
At the beginning of last year, it undertook its internationalisation process, with the establishment of an office in Paris.
Audatex was born in Germany in 1966 and established itself in Spain in 1979.
According to the company’s sources, 62% of its turnover is in Europe and the rest of the world and the remaining 38% in North and South America; it works with 900 insurance companies and 33,000 garages, of which 3 000 are located in Spain.
Solera’s global turnover (Audatex, Sidexa, Informex AB, Hollander and IMS) will reach USD 557 million, 3.3% higher than in 2008.
In our country, the company will receive EUR 19.1 million, an increase of 7.2% compared to last year.
In 2005, the Solera Group was publicly traded at USD 1 000 million, its equities are now valued at USD 2,1400 million.
Since 2007, it is on the New York Stock Exchange and is part of the Standard and Poor's Bench benchmark.
Auction on the Internet of damaged cars
Last October, the Solera Group bought Autoonline, an Internet platform for the sale of cars, motorbikes and targeted special vehicles.
The transaction involved a cost of EUR 59.5 million for 85% of the company's capital, which could be extended next year to the remaining 15%.
The objective of the acquisition was to offer more value-added services to its customers, with the possibility of gaining access to a huge international market.
This estimation and sales service is aimed exclusively at professionals in the sector, such as insurance companies, experts, leasing companies, garages, casings and professional vendors who must be registered before they start trading.
This platform was created in 1996 in Germany and manages more than 650,000 operations throughout Europe, including 500,000 in Germany.
Its main markets include Spain, Greece, Poland and Turkey.
More than 1,500 sellers and 4,000 experts are currently registered on the site.
It handles 3,000 advertisements per day on the Spanish market, 100 new advertisements are published every day.
In accordance with Spanish law, a car whose chassis number has been withdrawn may no longer circulate, but on the other hand, certain parts which are not damaged may be used as spare parts.
Collecting cars can be re-recorded, although they have been withdrawn, after complex steps.
Garage quotation in four steps
With the Auda-Taller tool, Audatex guarantees that the user obtains a quote in just four steps: identifying the vehicle, searching for the spare part, creating a quotation and generating it.
Utilisation is an essential element of these systems, especially in order to convince the oldest professionals who, to a greater or lesser extent, are resistant to the use of new management techniques.
The database comprises 1,034 vehicles, including 666 passenger cars, 109 off-road, 137 motorcycles, 78 vans and 44 lorries.
Of course, a computer and an Internet connection are required for use.
Once the model to be repaired has been identified through its chassis number, an actual graphics image of the model in question appears and the necessary part is selected.
The user may include variables such as the price of the labour force, mark-ups or discounts, and he can print the quotation to hand it over to the customer.
AudaTaller is not an estimation tool like AudaPlus, but a catalog of references.
The main difference between these two systems is that the one intended for garages does not include estimated manpower times.
The cost of using this garage tool is EUR 350 per year, which can be credited.
Currently, Audatex is currently working to ensure that these images are in three dimensions.
It is an innovation that mechanics see in a very positive light, since it will make it possible, for example, to select a gate and rotate it to see the inner face.
